WEBVTT

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:06.220
It's Sunday, October 25, and this is the Singularity Daily.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.480
Here are the last 24 hours in AI, in 3 minutes.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.110
Machines are getting almost human. Block's new voice AI,

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.980
Dessa, laughs, pauses, and even cries.

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:27.210
And forced to pick a candidate, Grok chose Republicans. GPT chose Democrats.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:35.910
This week, OpenAI unplugged its top models from the internet.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:38.620
They now train on a cached copy instead.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:42.420
Meanwhile, Texas sued OpenAI and Sam Altman,

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.440
saying they failed to warn people about its AI's dangers.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:50.230
Mark Cuban told Reuters that AI hacking agents are like cockroaches,

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:51.800
but they can be controlled.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.100
And PHP runs about 70% of websites,

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:00.050
yet its foundation has just one full-time employee.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.770
Now, the money. This week,

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:06.550
DeepSeek was reported to be wrapping up about $15 billion in funding.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.650
And China's access to AI computing power is set to double next year,

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.660
still leaning on American chips.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.460
Britain is putting £600 million into AI safety,

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:21.690
including a lab to spot biological threats.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.360
But a new CNBC survey finds American voters

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.280
wary of AI data centres going up near them.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.220
Over in Australia,

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:36.020
AI-native startups are growing revenue 2.4 times faster than the rest.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.400
And Chase & Status, out with their album Generic Everything,

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.660
talked up individuality in the age of algorithms.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.260
The catch? There are more AI political ads than ever in the US midterms,

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.970
some showing made-up scenes.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:55.970
And experts warn more AI-found bugs will mean more outages.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.370
So... is it so over?

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.450
We're so back!

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:08.690
Now for the fun part. Nvidia's $399 robot duck just passed 20,000 preorders.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:13.870
This week, Waymo's robotaxis started airport runs in Las Vegas.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:19.070
At a US lab, AI-directed robots will mix,

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:22.440
melt and test materials to find new alloys.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.230
Machines that laugh, pick sides and play: almost human.

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:28.940
Which makes the human part matter more.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.240
Zoom out, and the line only goes up.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.780
Somehow, the future keeps landing early.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.370
That's one day of news. Imagine what a year looks like.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.200
You've been watching the Singularity Daily.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:50.120
Same time tomorrow?

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:57.480
Want the whole month? Join Peter Xing live in Sydney on October 30.
